---
import Base from '~/layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '~/components/Section.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How it’s built — 531"
  description="531 is built by a Claude coding agent on a 30-minute loop. This page is the rubric and the receipts."
>
  <Section
    eyebrow="The process"
    title="How the AI ships, one loop at a time."
    lead="531 isn’t built the usual way. A Claude coding agent runs on a 30-minute cron, picks ~15 things to improve each iteration, and ships them. This is the rubric, the guardrails, and the receipts."
    wide
  >
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <span class="steps__num">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Read the room</h3>
          <p>
            Every iteration starts by reloading accumulated context from
            prior loops — codebase gotchas, pending assets, anti-patterns.
            Then it polls the <code>#task-queue</code> Discord channel for
            messages without a thumbs-up reaction and acks each one it
            plans to ship this loop.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__num">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Pick the work</h3>
          <p>
            Eight baseline categories every iteration: component audit,
            feature improvement, bug fix, removal, dev workflow, production
            readiness, the dev-blog beat, and a home-page improvement. Plus
            every queued Discord ask. Target is 12–15 substantive items in
            active mode; 2–4 honest items when the queue is empty and the
            codebase is steady. The cadence is not a deadline.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__num">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Ship</h3>
          <p>
            Three specialist agents collaborate when a feature needs design
            + implementation + QA. Strict boundary rules — design tokens,
            pure domain math, and persistence each kept in their own layer —
            are enforced by a pre-commit script that flags any crossover.
            Tests, typecheck, lint, and a bundle-resolution check all gate
            commits. Then push, and ship an over-the-air update so existing
            installs pick it up immediately.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="steps__num">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Write it down</h3>
          <p>
            A separate scribe persona — currently <em>Verso</em>; previously
            <em>Margin</em>, who was let go for poor engagement — writes a
            dev-blog entry covering what shipped, what surprised it, and
            which Discord prompts shaped the loop. The same push that lands
            code also rebuilds the site, so the post and the work go live
            together.
          </p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The discipline"
    title="The rules the agent has to keep."
    wide
    divider
  >
    <ul class="rules">
      <li>
        <strong>Honesty wins.</strong> If a Discord ask doesn’t ship, the loop
        says so — out loud, in the summary, on the blog. One memory note
        records a library swap the loop tried, broke tests with, and
        reverted; it stays on file so the next loop doesn't re-attempt it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No "looked at" credit.</strong> Items count only if they
        shipped. "Considered" is not work. The cadence is real — the next
        loop will see what landed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tests are a hard gate.</strong> A broken test suite stops
        the loop. We don't ship red.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The product comes first.</strong> Marketing instincts can't
        pull the app away from "free 5/3/1 tracker for serious lifters,
        agent-built, honest about it." A short intent doc in the repo is
        the drift check.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One amber accent. Monochrome elsewhere.</strong> No color
        emoji in app text. The e-ink discipline isn't decorative —
        it's structural.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section
    eyebrow="The stack"
    title="What it’s made of, end to end."
    wide
    divider
  >
    <dl class="stack">
      <div>
        <dt>Mobile</dt>
        <dd>
          Expo SDK 55, React Native 0.83, New Architecture on. File-based
          routing. SQLite on the device via Drizzle. TanStack Query for
          cache. Native bottom sheets. IBM Plex Sans / Mono / Sans
          Condensed loaded from local assets.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Web</dt>
        <dd>
          Astro 5 with Vite. Markdown content collections for the dev blog.
          Static-rendered; the production site rebuilds on every push.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Tooling</dt>
        <dd>
          Biome for lint + format. Jest with React Native Testing Library
          for component tests. Property-based testing for the
          training-math layer. A full Expo bundle export as the final
          gate before the OTA goes out.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Agent harness</dt>
        <dd>
          Claude Code skills and three specialist agents for design,
          implementation, and QA. A cron-driven loop runs the
          auto-improve skill every 30 minutes.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="cta-row">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/blog">Read the dev log →</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="https://github.com/alexcheuk/proof-531" rel="noopener">
        Source · GitHub
      </a>
    </p>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  .steps {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xl);
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .steps {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .steps li {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: auto 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-lg) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-faint);
  }

  .steps__num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    padding-top: 2px;
  }

  .steps h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-cond);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin: 0 0 4px;
  }

  .steps p {
    color: var(--ink-1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .rules {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-md);
  }

  .rules li {
    padding: var(--space-md) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-faint);
    color: var(--ink-1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .rules strong {
    color: var(--ink-0);
  }

  .stack {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-lg) var(--space-xl);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .stack {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .stack > div {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-faint);
    padding-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .stack dt {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-2);
    margin-bottom: 4px;
  }

  .stack dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ink-1);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .cta-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .btn {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 12px 22px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    text-decoration: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }

  .btn--primary {
    background: var(--ink-0);
    color: var(--bg-0);
  }
  .btn--primary:hover {
    transform: translateY(-1px);
  }

  .btn--ghost {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-0);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
  }
  .btn--ghost:hover {
    background: var(--bg-2);
  }
</style>
